WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.240
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:10.580
Approximately a 150 episodes ago, we looked
at DeepMind's amazing algorithm that was able

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:16.870
to look at a database with images of birds,
and it could learn about them so much that

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:22.000
we could provide a text description of an
imaginary bird type and it would dream up

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.730
new images of them.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:28.430
It was a truly breathtaking piece of work,
and its main limitation was that it could

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.270
only come up with coarse images.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:32.730
It didn’t give us a lot of details.

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:38.519
Later, we talked about NVIDIA's algorithm
that started out with such a coarse image,

00:00:38.519 --> 00:00:44.620
but didn't stop there - it progressively recomputed
this image many times, each time with more

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:46.120
and more details.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:50.640
This was able to create imaginary celebrities
with tons of detail.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.260
This new work offers a number of valuable
improvements over the previous techniques:

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:00.579
it can train bigger neural networks with even
more parameters, create extremely detailed

00:01:00.579 --> 00:01:06.010
images with remarkable performance, so much
so that if you have a reasonably powerful

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.800
graphics card, you can run it yourself here.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.060
The link is in the video description.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:16.030
Training these neural networks is also more
stable than it used to be with previous techniques.

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:21.110
As a result, it not only supports creating
these absolutely beautiful images, but also

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:25.680
gives us the opportunity to exert artistic
control on the outputs.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.230
I think this is super fun, I could play with
this all day long.

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:34.520
What's more, we can also interpolate between
these images, which means that if we have

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:41.300
desirable images A and B, it can compute intermediate
images between them, and the challenging part

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:46.490
is that these intermediate images shouldn't
be some sort of average between the two, which

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:51.740
would be gibberish, but they have to be images
that are meaningful, and can stand on their

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:52.760
own.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:53.760
Look at this!

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.530
Flying colors.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:57.480
And now comes the best part.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.040
The results were measured in terms of their
inception score.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:07.060
This inception score defines how recognizable
and diverse these generated images are and

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:13.290
most importantly, both of these are codified
in a mathematical manner to reduce the subjectivity

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:14.980
of the evaluation.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:19.689
This score is not perfect by any means, but
it typically correlates well with the scores

00:02:19.689 --> 00:02:21.189
given by humans.

00:02:21.189 --> 00:02:25.299
The best of the earlier works had an inception
score of around 50.

00:02:25.299 --> 00:02:32.049
And hold on to your papers, because the score
of this new technique is no less than 166,

00:02:32.049 --> 00:02:37.330
and if we would measure real images, they
would score around 233.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:40.159
What an incredible leap in technology.

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:45.060
And we are even being paid for creating and
playing with such learning algorithms.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:46.060
What a time to be alive!

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:51.020
A big thumbs up for the authors of the paper
for providing quite a bit of information on

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:52.709
failure cases as well.

00:02:52.709 --> 00:02:55.829
We also thank Insilico Medicine for supporting
this video.

00:02:55.829 --> 00:03:01.660
They are using these amazing learning algorithms
to create new molecules, identify new protein

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:06.790
targets with the aim to cure diseases and
aging itself.

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:09.310
Make sure to check them out in the video description.

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:13.290
They are our first sponsors, and it's been
such a joy to work with them.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.949
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

